---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import { GRAVITY, STEP as GRAVITY_STEP, BOUNCE } from '../../scripts/gravity/engine';
import { TICK_HZ, SEGMENTS } from '../../scripts/snake/engine';

const project = (await getEntry('projects', 'this-site'))!;

// Numbers quoted below that live in files we can't import at build time (WGSL, wipe) are named by file.
const stack = [
  { label: 'Framework', value: 'Astro 7, static output, zero JavaScript framework on the client' },
  { label: 'Language', value: 'TypeScript (strict) and plain CSS — no React, no Tailwind' },
  { label: 'Graphics', value: 'WebGPU (fluid + light), WebGL2 (liquid text), three.js (hero windows)' },
  { label: 'Type', value: 'Inter Tight and JetBrains Mono, self-hosted and preloaded' },
  { label: 'Hosting', value: 'GitHub Pages, deployed by GitHub Actions on every push to main' },
];
---

<ProjectLayout project={project}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose case" data-reveal>
      <h2>Why it's a project</h2>
      <p>
        Most of my portfolio's code isn't in the projects it shows — it's in the pages themselves. I built the
        site to find out how far I could push a static page before it stopped feeling like one, while keeping it
        fast and usable for everyone. This is a tour of what's under it. If you'd rather read the source, it's
        <a href={project.data.repo}>on GitHub</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Stack</h2>
      <dl class="stack">
        {
          stack.map((s) => (
            <div class="stack__row">
              <dt class="label">{s.label}</dt>
              <dd>{s.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p>
        Every page is HTML at build time. The interactive parts are small vanilla TypeScript modules that start on
        <code>astro:page-load</code> and clean up on <code>astro:before-swap</code>, so they survive the client-side
        router without leaking.
      </p>

      <h2>Two surfaces</h2>
      <p>
        There's no accent colour. The whole page eases between a warm dark and a warm white as you scroll: each
        top-level block is tagged with the surface it wants, and an <code>IntersectionObserver</code> watching a thin
        band a quarter of the way down the viewport flips a single attribute on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>. Every colour
        on the site is a CSS custom property registered with <code>@property</code>, so the browser can animate the
        change itself.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram" data-reveal>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 200" width="640" height="200" role="img" aria-labelledby="d-surface-title">
          <title id="d-surface-title">A page with dark and light blocks; the band at 25% of the viewport decides the current surface</title>
          <rect x="20" y="20" width="240" height="160" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.5" />
          <rect x="21" y="21" width="238" height="52" rx="6" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.85" />
          <rect x="21" y="75" width="238" height="60" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.08" />
          <rect x="21" y="137" width="238" height="42" rx="6" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.85" />
          <line x1="12" y1="60" x2="268" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-dasharray="4 4" />
          <text x="276" y="64" font-size="12" font-family="var(--font-mono)" fill="currentColor">25% band</text>
          <text x="300" y="100" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.75">block under the band → html[data-surface]</text>
          <text x="300" y="124" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.75">→ eight @property colours transition (0.5 s)</text>
          <text x="300" y="148" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.75">→ fixed layers read the live values each frame</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption class="label">surface.ts and the tokens in global.css</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The hero windows</h2>
      <p>
        The floating windows behind my name are a three.js scene. It isn't loaded with the page: the home page
        waits for the first sign of a visitor (a pointer move, a scroll, a key) and only then downloads three.js,
        so the first paint has no 3D in it at all. Each window's text is a canvas texture drawn with the same fonts
        as the page. The facts card you see first is real HTML; when the scene is ready each fact pops out as a
        lookalike element, flies onto its 3D twin and cross-fades into WebGL. Phones, data-saver connections and
        low-end devices keep the HTML card and never download the scene.
      </p>

      <h2>Fluid and light</h2>
      <p>
        On the home page, with WebGPU available, a fluid simulation runs behind everything. It's the classic
        "stable fluids" method on a grid of 6-pixel cells: advect the velocity field, splat the cursor into it,
        then make it incompressible with twenty Jacobi iterations of a pressure solve. The text and cards on the
        page are obstacles — the fluid flows around the words.
      </p>
      <p>
        The light is a 2D radiance cascade: five cascades of rays, each covering a longer interval than the last,
        so near light is sharp and far light is cheap. The cursor is a lamp. On dark it adds glow; on the light
        surface only its shadows show, as ink cast by the page's boxes. The tint is capped at 14% on dark and 10%
        on light (<code>shaders.ts</code>), which is the most it can do before muted text would drop below a 4.5:1
        contrast ratio.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram" data-reveal>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 220" width="640" height="220" role="img" aria-labelledby="d-cascade-title">
          <title id="d-cascade-title">Radiance cascades: rings of rays around a probe, each ring longer and sparser than the last</title>
          <g transform="translate(130 110)" stroke="currentColor" fill="none">
            <circle r="4" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" />
            <g stroke-opacity="0.9">
              {Array.from({ length: 8 }, (_, i) => { const a = (i / 8) * Math.PI * 2; return <line x1={Math.cos(a) * 6} y1={Math.sin(a) * 6} x2={Math.cos(a) * 18} y2={Math.sin(a) * 18} />; })}
            </g>
            <g stroke-opacity="0.65">
              {Array.from({ length: 16 }, (_, i) => { const a = (i / 16) * Math.PI * 2 + 0.1; return <line x1={Math.cos(a) * 20} y1={Math.sin(a) * 20} x2={Math.cos(a) * 42} y2={Math.sin(a) * 42} />; })}
            </g>
            <g stroke-opacity="0.4">
              {Array.from({ length: 32 }, (_, i) => { const a = (i / 32) * Math.PI * 2 + 0.05; return <line x1={Math.cos(a) * 44} y1={Math.sin(a) * 44} x2={Math.cos(a) * 92} y2={Math.sin(a) * 92} />; })}
            </g>
          </g>
          <text x="270" y="70" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.75">cascade 0 — short rays, dense probes</text>
          <text x="270" y="110" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.75">cascade 1 — 2× longer, 2× more directions, ¼ the probes</text>
          <text x="270" y="150" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.75">… five cascades reach about 500 cells</text>
          <text x="270" y="190" font-size="12" font-family="var(--font-mono)" fill="currentColor">field/index.ts · CASCADES = 5 · CELL = 6</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption class="label">Near light is sharp and far light is cheap</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The field stops simulating 3.5 seconds after the last input, and it's off entirely on touch devices, under
        <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, on data-saver connections and when there's no WebGPU. The page is
        exactly the same without it, just stiller.
      </p>

      <h2>Two C programs in the page</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/projects/wingravity">WinGravity</a> demo runs a line-for-line TypeScript port of the C physics
        loop: the same gravity ({GRAVITY} px/s²), the same bounce ({BOUNCE}), the same fixed timestep of
        {GRAVITY_STEP} s with an accumulator. Pointer events stand in for Win32 window hooks and CSS
        <code>translate</code> stands in for <code>SetWindowPos</code>. The <a href="/projects/procedural-animation">
        procedural animation</a> is the same idea: a {SEGMENTS}-circle chain stepped {TICK_HZ} times a second so the
        per-frame lerp means what it meant in raylib. Keeping the constants identical is the point — the demos
        are the programs, not sketches of them.
      </p>

      <h2>Motion</h2>
      <p>
        Pages don't swap, they wipe: on a click the next page is fetched while a panel covers the screen
        (450 ms), then the panel lifts off the new page (650 ms) — <code>wipe.ts</code>. Going from a project row to its
        page is the one exception: the title morphs across with a native view transition instead. Headings rise
        in by word or letter, sections reveal as they enter, big text ripples under the cursor on a shared WebGL2
        canvas, buttons drift toward a nearby pointer, and project titles swell in weight letter by letter using
        Inter Tight's variable axis.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram" data-reveal>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 120" width="640" height="120" role="img" aria-labelledby="d-wipe-title">
          <title id="d-wipe-title">Timeline of a page wipe: cover for 450 milliseconds while the next page loads, then reveal for 650 milliseconds</title>
          <line x1="20" y1="60" x2="620" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.3" />
          <rect x="20" y="40" width="190" height="40" rx="6" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.85" />
          <text x="30" y="65" font-size="12" font-family="var(--font-mono)" fill="var(--bg)">cover · 450 ms</text>
          <rect x="20" y="86" width="190" height="4" rx="2" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.35" />
          <text x="30" y="108" font-size="11" font-family="var(--font-mono)" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.7">fetch + swap in parallel</text>
          <rect x="220" y="40" width="270" height="40" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
          <text x="230" y="65" font-size="12" font-family="var(--font-mono)" fill="currentColor">reveal · 650 ms</text>
          <text x="500" y="65" font-size="12" font-family="var(--font-mono)" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.7">→ headings rise</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption class="label">The label on the panel is the next page's name</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        All of it respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>: with it on, there are no wipes, no parallax, no
        smooth scroll, no ripples and no field, and the content simply appears.
      </p>

      <h2>Speed</h2>
      <p>
        The bar is Lighthouse 97–100 on every page, desktop and mobile, with zero layout shift. The things that
        make that possible: no framework runtime, fonts preloaded and self-hosted, three.js in its own chunk that
        only the home page loads and only after you move, the WebGPU field loaded after the hero has settled,
        images resized at build time, pages prefetched on hover so the wipe never waits on the network, and
        colour variables kept off <code>:root</code> unless everything needs them (inherited properties make full-page
        restyles slow). Things I tried and removed: <code>mix-blend-mode</code> and <code>backdrop-filter</code> on
        fixed layers, and generated environment maps in the hero, which froze the page for half a second.
      </p>

      <h2>Accessibility</h2>
      <p>
        Semantic HTML first: real headings, lists and landmarks, a skip link, visible focus rings. Split headings
        keep an <code>aria-label</code> with the whole phrase; letter-by-letter titles keep a screen-reader copy.
        The custom cursor and hover cards are mouse-only; touch gets native behaviour. The lightest text colour
        fails contrast, so it's only ever decoration. Every page passes an axe scan.
      </p>

      <h2>Deployment</h2>
      <figure class="diagram" data-reveal>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 90" width="640" height="90" role="img" aria-labelledby="d-deploy-title">
          <title id="d-deploy-title">Pipeline: Markdown and TypeScript go through Astro's build into static HTML, then GitHub Actions deploys to GitHub Pages at alby.software</title>
          <g font-size="12" font-family="var(--font-mono)" fill="currentColor">
            <rect x="10" y="25" width="120" height="40" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
            <text x="22" y="50">md + .astro + ts</text>
            <line x1="130" y1="45" x2="160" y2="45" stroke="currentColor" />
            <rect x="160" y="25" width="120" height="40" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
            <text x="183" y="50">astro build</text>
            <line x1="280" y1="45" x2="310" y2="45" stroke="currentColor" />
            <rect x="310" y="25" width="120" height="40" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
            <text x="331" y="50">static HTML</text>
            <line x1="430" y1="45" x2="460" y2="45" stroke="currentColor" />
            <rect x="460" y="25" width="170" height="40" rx="6" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.85" />
            <text x="476" y="50" fill="var(--bg)">Pages · alby.software</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption class="label">.github/workflows/deploy.yml, on every push to main</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        There's no server. A push to <code>main</code> runs the Astro build in GitHub Actions and publishes the output
        to GitHub Pages under a custom domain. Share images are rendered at build time, the sitemap and RSS feed are
        generated, and the whole thing is a folder of files.
      </p>

      <h2>Credits</h2>
      <p>
        Astro, three.js, Lenis for smooth scrolling, Shiki for code colours, Satori for the share images; Inter
        Tight by Rasmus Andersson and JetBrains Mono by JetBrains. The radiance cascades idea is Alexander
        Sannikov's. Everything else is mine, including the mistakes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
  .case {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4rem);
  }

  .stack {
    margin: 0 0 1.5em;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .stack__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .stack__row dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .diagram {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .diagram svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-width: 40rem;
  }

  .diagram figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .stack__row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.2rem;
    }
  }
</style>
